novelty_contribution:
{
  "theoretical_contribution": "This study introduces a novel theoretical mechanism linking model interpretability, perceived transparency, and clinician trust within a regulatory framework, grounded in dual-process theory. It elucidates how interpretability mediates the relationship between model outputs and clinician decision-making efficiency in chronic disease diagnosis.",
  "technical_contribution": "The research develops and evaluates a hybrid human-AI framework that integrates interpretable LLMs with Explainable AI techniques, such as attention visualization and feature importance mapping, specifically tailored for clinical decision support. This approach enhances the understanding of internal representations and their impact on clinician trust.",
  "empirical_contribution": "Through a controlled between-subject design, the study provides empirical evidence on the effects of interpretable versus non-interpretable LLMs on clinician trust and decision-making efficiency, utilizing trust calibration measures and task performance metrics. This contributes new insights into the dynamics of AI trust in high-stakes healthcare settings.",
  "practical_deployment_contribution": "The findings will inform deployment guidelines for integrating interpretable LLMs in clinical environments, emphasizing the importance of regulatory compliance and user trust. This practical contribution addresses the critical need for ethical AI applications in healthcare, ensuring that model outputs align with clinician needs and regulatory standards."
}

